  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1144 AM MDT Sun Aug 16 2026\n\nResidual, but limited moisture may result in a shower mainly \nalong the Divide this afternoon. Otherwise most locations will \nbe dry. The same can be said about tomorrow. A weak frontal \nboundary sets up across the Northern Great Basin Tuesday into \nWednesday, tightening up the surface pressure gradient across \nnortheast Utah and northwest Colorado. Some gustier winds of \n20-30 mph will be possible in this region, although surface \nrelative humidity values will be high enough that critical fire \nweather conditions are not anticipated. Some extra moisture \naccompanies this frontal boundary, with PWAT values forecast to \nbe 100-150% of normal across the region, with the best moisture \nnorth of I-70. With a combination of orographics and a bit of \nforcing from the frontal boundary, some isolated to widely \nscattered showers and thunderstorms will be possible across the \nhigher terrain Tuesday and Wednesday afternoon. Slightly above \nnormal moisture lingers through the end of the work week, \nleading to lower-end (20-30%) chances of showers or \nthunderstorms over the higher terrain each afternoon. The main \nthreats with these storms will be gusty outflow winds and \nlightning, although a brief heavy downpour cannot be ruled out. \n\nHigh pressure settles over the Four Corners Thursday into Friday, \nushering in a drying and warming trend. By the end of the week, high \ntemperatures are expected to run around 10 degrees above normal, \nwith triple digits across the lower desert valleys and the Four \nCorners. Friday into the weekend, models are indicating a trough \nmoving into the Pacific Northwest will nudge the high eastward once \nagain, returning weak monsoonal flow into the Four Corners.",
